Leasing doesn't fix your problem, and it makes you dearer.

**It doesn't close the gap.** A lessor normally pays you when the customer accepts the system, and for you that's go-live. You'd still carry the same four to nine months (judgement; check with any lessor you talk to).

**It's a poor asset for a lessor.** Sensors built into a customer's site are hard to take back and resell. Lessors either price that risk high or decline (judgement).

**It makes your offer dearer.** The customer pays the lessor's margin and has to pass a credit check, so deals slow down. Moving the cost onto the buyer is the weaker answer. The better one is the public side carrying the risk on your own line (judgement).

**Where leasing does fit:** as a sales option for customers who would rather pay monthly. It should sit beside your own financing, not replace it.

**Better on the customer side:** a premium that pays for part of the kit. In Germany, some statutory accident insurers pay safety bonuses for person-detection systems. The construction one does this for camera-based detection on building machinery, with a tested-system requirement (unverified, [BG BAU requirements](https://www.bgbau.de/fileadmin/Produkte/Arbeitsschutzpraemie/Kamerabasierte_Personenerkennungsysteme_Anforderungen.pdf)). Whether the trade-and-logistics insurer does the same for forklifts I haven't confirmed. Their prevention team can say. If it does, that's a selling point that costs you nothing.

So the priority holds: the Danish line first, the Munich line second, and EIFO tested in the round before close.

